## 3. Telephone parsing — country code / national number / `+61` / `+1` logic

The single source of truth is:
`/home/daniel/sandbox/src/main/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/util/TelephoneNormalizer.java`
```java
public final class TelephoneNormalizer {
    /** Country code assumed when the number carries no explicit '+' prefix. */
    private static final String DEFAULT_COUNTRY_CODE = "61";
    private static final Pattern SEPARATORS = Pattern.compile("[\\s()\\-]");
    private static final int MIN_DIGITS = 8;
    private static final int MAX_DIGITS = 15;

    private TelephoneNormalizer() {}

    public static Optional<String> toE164(String telephone) {
        if (telephone == null) {
            return Optional.empty();
        }
        String cleaned = SEPARATORS.matcher(telephone).replaceAll("");
        String digits;
        if (cleaned.startsWith("+")) {
            digits = cleaned.substring(1);
        } else {
            String national = cleaned.startsWith("0") ? cleaned.substring(1) : cleaned;
            digits = DEFAULT_COUNTRY_CODE + national;
        }
        if (digits.length() < MIN_DIGITS || digits.length() > MAX_DIGITS || !isAllDigits(digits)) {
            return Optional.empty();
        }
        return Optional.of("+" + digits);
    }

    private static boolean isAllDigits(String value) {
        for (int i = 0; i < value.length(); i++) {
            char c = value.charAt(i);
            if (c < '0' || c > '9') return false;
        }
        return true;
    }
}
```
Key behaviours:
- Strips spaces, dashes, parentheses.
- If it starts with `+`, the country code is kept as-is (so `+1...` NANP numbers pass through unchanged) — total digits after `+` must be 8-15.
- Otherwise assumes `+61` (Australia) and drops a single leading `0` from national digits.
- **Note:** it only checks a total digit count of 8-15; it does **not** enforce any per-country national-number length (e.g. it does not require `+61` to have exactly 9 national digits). The acceptance test in section 4 asserts exactly such a per-country rule, which this normalizer does not currently implement.

